{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much down payment do I need to buy a house in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "How much you put down depends on the loan program, whether the home will be your homestead or an investment property, and what your lender's overlay requires. Conventional, FHA and VA each set different minimums, and a veteran with full entitlement has no VA loan limit. Only a lender preapproval gives you a real number."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does a Texas investment property require a larger down payment than a primary residence?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. Occupancy is the single largest factor in the required down payment, larger than credit score. Primary residence, second home and investment property are underwritten as three different risks. Occupancy is a written representation to the lender, and misrepresenting it is mortgage fraud."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do VA loans in Texas have a loan limit?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A veteran with full entitlement has no VA loan limit, and the VA guaranty does not exceed 25 percent of the loan. That is a VA rule, not a lender promise — the lender still underwrites income, assets, credit and the appraisal. A veteran with partial entitlement is in a different position, which the Certificate of Eligibility settles."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What cash do I need besides the down payment in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The option fee and earnest money, both delivered to the title company early in the contract; inspection and due diligence costs paid during the option period; closing costs, which are negotiated in the contract rather than fixed by law; and documented reserves left in the account after closing, which many lender overlays require."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How does Texas homestead law affect how much I should put down?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Borrowing against a Texas homestead is capped by the Texas Constitution, Section 50(a)(6), at 80 percent of fair market value, with a 12-day waiting period, a 2 percent cap on most fees, non-recourse treatment, and only one such loan at a time. If the plan is to pull cash out later, that cap sets the ceiling. Investment property is not governed by 50(a)(6)."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
