      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Hill Country land is not a passive investment. The numbers that decide it are specific to the tract: whether it carries 1-d-1 open-space ag valuation, what a change of use would trigger in rollback taxes for the prior three years, legal access and easements, groundwater availability under Cow Creek GCD rules, and septic feasibility."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How does a Texas ag exemption work on Hill Country land?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Under 1-d-1 open-space appraisal, land is valued on productivity value rather than market value. It requires current principal agricultural use, use to the degree of intensity generally accepted in the area, and agricultural or timber production in 5 of the preceding 7 years. Wildlife management use is available on previously qualified land and requires at least 3 of 7 specified activities."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the rollback tax when land comes out of ag use in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Texas Tax Code 23.55 imposes an additional tax equal to the difference between the taxes imposed on the land for each of the three years preceding the year in which the change of use occurs. The rollback is triggered by the change of use, not the sale, so the buyer who changes the use inherits the bill."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is there an interest rate on Texas rollback taxes?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Texas Tax Code 23.55 does not impose a separate rollback interest rate. It provides only that the additional taxes incur penalties and interest as provided by law for ad valorem taxes imposed by the taxing unit if they are not paid by the delinquency deadline. Articles quoting a specific rollback interest percentage are not citing the statute."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What should I check before buying land in Kendall County, Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Current ag valuation status and rollback exposure with the appraisal district, groundwater and well permitting with the Cow Creek Groundwater Conservation District, recorded legal access and easements with a Texas real estate attorney, a survey, the cost to extend electric service, septic feasibility, severed mineral rights, and any deed restrictions or POA. Raw land financing is also its own product and should be arranged before writing an offer."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is the option fee refundable in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. The option fee is not refundable. If the buyer terminates during the option period, the seller keeps it — that is what the fee bought. If the sale closes, the fee is credited to the sales price, so the buyer effectively gets it back at the closing table. Earnest money is the refundable deposit."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do you get the option fee back at closing in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Effectively, yes. Paragraph 5A(4) of TREC form 20-19 states the option fee will be credited to the Sales Price at closing, so it appears as a credit on the buyer's side of the settlement statement and reduces the cash needed at the table. It is a credit, not a refund."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between the option fee and earnest money in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The option fee buys the unrestricted right to terminate and becomes the seller's money. Earnest money is a good-faith deposit that stays the buyer's money in escrow. Paragraph 5B of TREC form 20-19 says that on a timely termination the option fee will not be refunded and any earnest money will be refunded to the buyer."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can the seller receive the option fee before closing in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. In paragraph 5A(4) the buyer authorizes the escrow agent to release and deliver the option fee to the seller at any time without further notice to or consent from the buyer. Earnest money cannot be released that way — it requires mutual agreement or a court order."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if the option fee is delivered late or left blank in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Either one forfeits the termination right. Paragraph 5D of TREC form 20-19 states that if no dollar amount is stated as the option fee, or if the buyer fails to deliver it within the time required, the buyer shall not have the unrestricted right to terminate under paragraph 5. Paragraph 5E adds that time is of the essence."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
