}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What should I know about moving from California to the Texas Hill Country?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Texas has no state individual income tax, and it funds local government largely through property tax instead. Texas property is reappraised at market value every year, unlike California under Proposition 13. In Boerne, Boerne ISD's 2025 rate was 1.0109 and the City of Boerne's was 0.4716 per $100 of taxable value."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How is Texas property tax different from California property tax?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Texas appraisal districts value property at market value and reappraise it annually. California's Proposition 13 limits reassessment. Once a Texas homestead exemption is in place there is a 10% per-year cap on the increase in net appraised value, but the cap limits how fast the value climbs, not where it ends up."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are the property tax rates in Boerne, Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "For 2025, per $100 of taxable value, a home inside the Boerne city limits in Boerne ISD sat under Boerne ISD 1.0109, City of Boerne 0.4716 and Kendall County 0.377 — a combined 1.8595 before any special district and before exemptions. Comfort ISD's 2025 rate was 0.9328. Some parcels also carry Kendall County MUD, Miralomas MUD, Cow Creek Groundwater Conservation District or Kendall County WCID #1, so pull the entity list for the specific parcel from Kendall Appraisal District."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "When can I claim a homestead exemption after moving to Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "You must have owned and occupied the home as your principal residence on January 1 of the tax year. A buyer who closes in February does not get the exemption that year and receives it the following year. The application deadline is before May 1, and the form is filed with the appraisal district, not by the title company."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much is the Texas school homestead exemption?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "School districts must grant a $140,000 residence homestead exemption, raised from $100,000 by Proposition 13, approved by Texas voters in November 2025 and implemented by SB 4 of the 89th Legislature, effective for tax year 2026. Owners age 65 or older, or disabled, receive an additional $60,000 from the school district."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the sales tax rate in Boerne, Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "8.25% total, which is the Texas maximum: 6.25% state, 1.50% City of Boerne and 0.50% Kendall County. Hotel occupancy tax on short-term stays runs 13% total, 6% state plus 7% city."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Should I buy inside Boerne city limits or in unincorporated Kendall County?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Inside the city limits you get City of Boerne utilities and ordinances and pay the city's 0.4716 rate. Unincorporated means well and septic, no city rate, and fewer local rules — except in Boerne's ETJ, where the city still regulates platting and some ordinances, such as short-term rentals, still reach you."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does Boerne's ETJ control?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The extraterritorial jurisdiction is unincorporated area outside the city limits where the city keeps regulatory authority, notably subdivision platting under Texas Local Government Code Chapter 42. Boerne's short-term rental ordinance also reaches the ETJ, although the STR safety inspection requirement does not apply there."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do I still pay school and county taxes outside Boerne city limits?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. Only the City of Boerne rate of 0.4716 drops off. Kendall County at 0.377 and the school district still apply, plus any special district such as Kendall County MUD, Miralomas MUD, Kendall County WCID #1 or the Cow Creek Groundwater Conservation District. Confirm the entity list per parcel with Kendall Appraisal District."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What extra diligence does an unincorporated Kendall County property need?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A tested well yield, well depth, casing and pump condition, septic type, capacity and permit history, any shared well or shared road agreement, groundwater district permitting and pumping limits, and the recorded deed restrictions. A standard home inspection does not cover the well or certify the septic system."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens to an agricultural valuation if I build on unincorporated acreage?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Changing the use of land under 1-d-1 open-space appraisal triggers a three-year rollback tax equal to the difference between taxes paid on agricultural value and taxes that would have been paid on market value. Ask Kendall Appraisal District whether the valuation survives your intended use and a CPA what the rollback would cost."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the Texas disabled veteran property tax exemption?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Texas gives disabled veterans a fixed dollar exemption off one property's appraised value, scaled to the VA disability rating and starting at $5,000 for a 10-29% rating. A veteran rated 100% disabled, or determined individually unemployable, gets a total exemption on their residence homestead under Tax Code 11.131."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does individual unemployability qualify for the Texas 100 percent veteran exemption?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. Under Tax Code 11.131, a veteran qualifies on either a 100 percent disability rating or a VA determination of individual unemployability. It is one or the other, not both. Veterans compensated at the 100 percent rate through unemployability often assume incorrectly that they do not qualify."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How late can a Texas disabled veteran file for the exemption?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The application is Form 50-114 with an ordinary April 30 deadline, but the 100 percent disabled veteran exemption may be filed up to five years after the delinquency date for the taxes in question. The appraisal district can say which years remain open on an account."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can a surviving spouse keep the disabled veteran exemption?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, if the spouse has not remarried, the property was the veteran's residence homestead at death, and it remains the spouse's homestead. If the surviving spouse moves, the exemption is capped at the dollar value of the last year's exemption on the prior home rather than being a full exemption on the new one."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are the Texas disabled veteran exemption amounts by rating?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Under Texas Tax Code 11.22(a): a rating of at least 10 percent but less than 30 percent gets $5,000; at least 30 but less than 50 percent gets $7,500; at least 50 but less than 70 percent gets $10,000; and 70 percent or more gets $12,000. The exemption applies to one property the veteran designates."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does the partial disabled veteran exemption apply to more than one property?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. The partial exemption under Tax Code 11.22 is a fixed dollar amount off the appraised value of one property the veteran owned on January 1, and it applies to that same property across all taxing units. The applicant must be a Texas resident to qualify for a homestead exemption."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the Texas over-65 property tax exemption and school tax ceiling?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Texas homeowners 65 or older get an additional $60,000 school district homestead exemption on top of the general $140,000, plus a school tax ceiling that freezes school taxes at the amount for the year they qualified. Moving transfers the ceiling as a percentage, not a dollar amount, and only if you file for it."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much is the Texas over-65 exemption?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "School districts must grant an additional $60,000 residence homestead exemption to owners 65 or older or disabled, on top of the general $140,000 school district exemption for 2026. Other taxing units may adopt a local option exemption of at least $3,000 for the same group."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does the Texas school tax ceiling transfer if I move?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, but as a percentage rather than a dollar amount, and only if you file for it. Request a tax ceiling certificate, Form 50-311, from the old appraisal district, file it with the new district, and file a new homestead application including the age-65 exemption."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does the over-65 ceiling freeze my whole tax bill?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. The mandatory ceiling covers the school district portion. City, county and special district freezes are local options that not every entity adopts. A total bill can still rise when a non-frozen entity raises its rate."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can the over-65 tax ceiling ever go up?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The ceiling can be raised to account for new improvements to the property, such as added square footage or a new structure. Ordinary repairs and maintenance are treated differently. The appraisal district decides how a specific project is classified."
      }
    }
  ]
}

