        "text": "For 2026 the conforming one-unit baseline is $832,750, with a high-cost ceiling of $1,249,125. FHA's nationwide one-unit floor is $541,287 and ceiling $1,249,125. Kendall County sits in the San Antonio-New Braunfels MSA, whose 2026 FHA one-unit limit is reported at $557,750 — confirm it on HUD's lookup. VA has no limit on full entitlement."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the 2026 conforming loan limit for a one-unit property?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "$832,750, up $26,250 from 2025's $806,500, with a high-cost area ceiling of $1,249,125. FHFA announced the values after third-quarter 2025 house price index data showed prices up 3.26 percent year over year. The baseline is the figure a Kendall County buyer plans against; the ceiling applies only in areas FHFA designates as high-cost."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "When do 2026 FHA loan limits take effect?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Per Mortgagee Letter 2025-23, the 2026 FHA limits apply to case numbers assigned January 1 through December 31, 2026. The trigger is the case number assignment date, not the contract date or the closing date, so a case number assigned in December 2025 falls under the 2025 limit."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if my loan is above the conforming limit?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "It becomes a jumbo loan. Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac will not buy it, so the individual lender or its private investor sets the down payment, reserve, credit and rate terms. Jumbo terms vary far more between lenders than conforming terms, because there is no common agency rulebook."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does a loan limit cap what house I can buy?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. A loan limit is a threshold in the loan amount, not the purchase price, and it governs whether the loan is agency-eligible rather than what a buyer can afford. A purchase above the limit can still be a conforming loan if the down payment brings the loan amount under it."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What credit score do I need to buy a house in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "There is no single number. Each loan program sets an agency minimum, and then the individual lender adds its own stricter overlay — the overlay is what you actually have to meet. Score also prices the loan and the mortgage insurance, not just approval. Get a real preapproval from a lender who pulls credit and reads your income documents."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is a lender overlay?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "An overlay is a requirement a lender adds on top of the agency program minimum, based on its own risk tolerance and investor relationships. Overlays are not published and vary between lenders. The overlay at the lender you apply with is the standard your file actually has to meet."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which credit score do mortgage lenders use?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Lenders pull a tri-merge report from all three bureaus, each with its own score, and standard practice is to use the middle of the three. On a joint application the lower borrower's middle score typically governs. The free score in a banking app is usually not the score that decides the loan."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does a higher credit score do anything besides get me approved?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. Score is primarily a pricing input. It affects the interest rate or closing costs through loan-level pricing adjustments on conventional loans, and it affects what private mortgage insurance costs. Two approvals on the same purchase can carry meaningfully different monthly payments."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What makes a preapproval strong in a Texas offer?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A preapproval where the lender has pulled credit, read income and asset documents, and run automated underwriting. An underwritten preapproval reviewed by a human underwriter is stronger still. The listing side weighs that letter together with the Third Party Financing Addendum, TREC form 40-11, effective 01/03/2025."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does the effective date mean in a Texas real estate contract?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The effective date is the date the last party signs the contract and that signature is delivered. It is day zero. Every other deadline in a Texas contract counts from it: the option fee delivery, the option period, the financing deadline, title objections and the closing date. A wrong effective date makes every one of them wrong."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is the effective date the date the buyer signed the offer?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. The effective date requires the last party to sign and that final signature to be delivered. The date the offer was written, the date it was emailed, a verbal acceptance, and the date earnest money was wired are all commonly mistaken for it and none of them are it."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which Texas contract deadlines run from the effective date?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Paragraph 5A requires the earnest money and option fee within 3 days after the Effective Date. Paragraph 5B counts the option period in days after the Effective Date, ending at 5:00 p.m. local time. Paragraph 6C sets the survey election days, Paragraph 7B the seller's disclosure delivery days, Paragraph 6D the title objection window, plus the financing deadline and closing."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if the effective date is left blank on a Texas contract?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No deadline can be computed. In practice the option fee gets delivered late, and Paragraph 5D states that if no dollar amount is stated as the option fee, or the buyer fails to deliver it within the time required, the buyer does not have the unrestricted right to terminate. Paragraph 5E adds that time is of the essence and strict compliance is required."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can you change the effective date on a Texas contract?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Changing a date on an executed contract changes a contract term, which requires an Amendment to Contract, TREC form 39-11, signed by every party and delivered. Backdating an effective date is a misstatement on an executed contract, not a scheduling convenience. If deadlines have already run, consult a Texas real estate attorney."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens at a Texas real estate closing?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "At a Texas closing the title company acts as escrow agent. The parties sign the deed, note and closing disclosure, the buyer wires funds, the lender funds the loan, and the title company records the deed. Signing is not closing — the deal closes when it funds and records. No attorney is required."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is signing the same as closing in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Signing is the execution of documents. Closing is funding and recording. Funds are not disbursed and documents are not recorded until the title company has received all required items, so a file can sit fully signed and still not be closed."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do you need a lawyer to close on a house in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. The title company acts as escrow agent, runs the title search, issues the commitment and policy, prepares closing documents and disburses funds. Hiring an attorney is optional, and worth it for title defects, probate or estate sales, entity purchases, boundary disputes or custom contract language."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "When do you get the keys in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "At closing and funding, per the contract, not at the signing appointment. If the parties want possession on a different schedule, that is a written agreement, such as a promulgated temporary residential lease."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if a Texas home is damaged before closing?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Under paragraph 14 of TREC form 20-19 the seller must restore the property by the Closing Date. If the seller cannot due to factors beyond the seller's control, the buyer may terminate and have the earnest money refunded, extend the time for performance up to 15 days, or accept the property damaged with an assignment of insurance proceeds plus a credit from the seller at closing in the amount of the deductible."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How are property taxes handled at a Texas closing?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Paragraph 13 of TREC form 20-19 prorates taxes for the current year, interest, rents and regular periodic maintenance fees, assessments and dues through the Closing Date. The proration may account for a change in exemptions, and if the actual taxes differ from the amount prorated, the parties adjust once the current year's tax statements are available."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is a 1031 exchange?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A 1031 exchange lets an owner defer capital gain on real property held for productive use in a trade or business or for investment by exchanging it for like-kind real property. You have 45 days to identify replacement property in writing and 180 days to receive it. A personal residence does not qualify."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are the 45-day and 180-day 1031 deadlines?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Both run from the transfer of the relinquished property. Replacement property must be identified within 45 days in writing, signed and delivered to a person involved in the exchange such as the qualified intermediary. The replacement property must be received within 180 days, or by the due date of the tax return including extensions, whichever is earlier."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why does a late-in-the-year closing threaten a 1031 exchange?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Because the receipt deadline is 180 days or the tax return due date including extensions, whichever is earlier. If the relinquished property closes in November, the return due date can arrive before the 180th day and cut the exchange period short. A CPA handles this before the deadline, usually with an extension."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I do a 1031 exchange on my home?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Section 1031 applies to real property held for productive use in a trade or business or for investment. A personal residence does not qualify. Since the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act, Section 1031 also applies only to real property, not to personal or intangible property."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is there a TREC form for a 1031 exchange?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. TREC publishes an Addendum for Section 1031 Exchange, form 60-0, effective 01/03/2025. It puts the other party on notice that the transaction is part of an exchange, but it does not replace the exchange documents prepared by the qualified intermediary. The exchange itself is reported to the IRS on Form 8824."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is a MUD or a PID in a Texas subdivision?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A MUD is a municipal utility district and a PID is a public improvement district. Both fund water, sewer, roads and amenities in a Texas subdivision, and both add a line to the property tax bill that can change the monthly payment. Each has a TREC notice form that goes to the buyer in the transaction."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between a MUD and a PID in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A MUD is a political subdivision with its own board that levies a property tax and typically owns water, wastewater and drainage infrastructure. A PID is created by a city or county to fund specified improvements and imposes an assessment rather than a tax. A PID assessment often has a payoff balance; a MUD tax generally does not."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "When must a seller give the MUD notice in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Texas Water Code Section 49.452 requires the seller to give written notice prior to execution of a binding contract of sale and purchase, and the purchaser signs to evidence receipt. If it was not given before the contract, the seller may give it at closing, but a purchaser who proceeds waives termination rights. The content of the notice is set by Section 49.4521."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does the Texas PID notice say?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Under Texas Property Code Section 5.014 the seller must give written notice before the effective date of a binding contract. It states that the buyer is obligated to pay assessments for public improvement costs, that AN ASSESSMENT HAS BEEN LEVIED AGAINST YOUR PROPERTY, the payment terms, and that failure to pay may result in penalties and interest being added to what you owe or in a lien on and the foreclosure of your property."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which TREC forms disclose a MUD or PID?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The Addendum Containing Notice of Obligation to Pay Improvement District Assessment, form 53-0 effective 09/01/2021, carries the Property Code 5.014 notice on the PID side. The Notice to Purchaser of Special Taxing or Assessment District, form 59-0 effective 02/12/2024, is the notice given to a purchaser of property inside a special taxing or assessment district."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Are there MUDs in Kendall County, Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Kendall Appraisal District's entity list includes a Kendall County MUD and a Miralomas MUD, along with Kendall County WCID #1 and the Cow Creek Groundwater Conservation District. Check the specific parcel's account at kendallad.org, because district boundaries do not follow subdivision names."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is a T-47 affidavit in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A T-47 is a notarized affidavit in which a Texas seller states that nothing has changed on the property since the existing survey. With it, the lender and title company can often accept that survey instead of requiring a new one. A T-47.1 declaration does the same job without notarization; both took effect November 1, 2024."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between a T-47 and a T-47.1 in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "They make the same substantive statements about the property being unchanged since the existing survey. The T-47 is a notarized affidavit. The T-47.1 is a declaration in lieu of affidavit, signed by the seller but not notarized. Both are effective November 1, 2024, and the title company and lender decide which they will accept."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Who signs the T-47 affidavit?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The seller. The seller identifies the existing survey and swears that no changes have been made to the property since it was made. The title insurance company issuing the policy relies on those statements, which is why an inaccurate affidavit is a serious problem rather than a paperwork error. Paragraph 6C(1) of TREC form 20-19 names both the T-47 Affidavit and the T-47.1 Declaration as the seller's obligation."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does a T-47 do for the title policy?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Paragraph 6A(8) of TREC form 20-19 lets the standard printed exception as to discrepancies, conflicts, shortages in area or boundary lines, encroachments or protrusions, or overlapping improvements be amended to read only 'shortages in area,' at Buyer's or Seller's expense. With an acceptable existing survey plus a T-47 or T-47.1, those matters are covered rather than excluded, and the buyer may not have to pay for a new survey."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if a Texas seller does not deliver the T-47 on time?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Paragraph 6C(1) of TREC form 20-19 requires the seller to furnish both the existing survey and the T-47 Affidavit or T-47.1 Declaration within the time stated. If the seller fails to furnish both, the buyer obtains a new survey at Seller's expense no later than 3 days prior to the Closing Date. There is no election on that branch."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is a Texas cash-out refinance under Section 50(a)(6)?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A Texas cash-out refinance is a home equity loan under Article XVI, Section 50(a)(6) of the Texas Constitution. All loans against the homestead combined cannot exceed 80 percent of fair market value, the loan cannot close until 12 days after the later of application or notice, and fees are capped at 2 percent."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much cash can I take out of a Texas home?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The ceiling is on the combined balance. All loans secured by the homestead together cannot exceed 80 percent of fair market value at closing, so the available proceeds are 80 percent of the appraised value minus everything already owed, minus closing costs. At least 20 percent equity must remain in the property."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is a Texas HELOC also a 50(a)(6) loan?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. A home equity line of credit secured by a Texas homestead is a Section 50(a)(6) loan and carries the same limits, including the 80 percent combined ceiling and the rule that only one such loan may be secured by the homestead at a time. An open HELOC therefore has to be addressed before a 50(a)(6) cash-out refinance."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How long does a Texas cash-out refinance take to close?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The loan cannot close until at least 12 days after the later of the application date or the borrower's receipt of the required notice. That waiting period is constitutional and cannot be waived or shortened by the lender, which is why Texas cash-out refinances often take longer than borrowers expect."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does Section 50(a)(6) apply to rental property in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Section 50(a)(6) governs the homestead. Investment property in Texas is not governed by these rules, so investor cash-out loans follow the lender's own loan-to-value, seasoning and reserve requirements instead. Whether a specific property is the homestead is a legal question for a Texas real estate attorney."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is a title commitment in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A title commitment is the title company's written promise to issue a title policy on stated terms. It comes in four schedules: A is the deal facts, B is what the policy will not cover, C is what must be fixed before closing, and D discloses who gets paid. Reading it is a task with a deadline."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is Schedule C on a Texas title commitment?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Schedule C lists the requirements that must be satisfied before the title company will issue the policy: lien releases, mortgage payoffs, probate or heirship documents, missing spousal signatures, unpaid taxes, corrected legal descriptions. Unworked Schedule C items are the most common reason a Texas closing date slips."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the survey exception in a Texas title policy?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "It is a Schedule B exception excluding anything a survey would have shown: boundary discrepancies, shortages in area, encroachments and protrusions. It can be amended with a new survey, or with the existing survey plus a signed T-47 affidavit, if the lender and title company accept it."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "When does the title commitment have to be delivered in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Paragraph 6B of TREC form 20-19 requires it within 20 days after the Title Company receives a copy of the contract, automatically extended up to 15 days or 3 days before the Closing Date, whichever is earlier. If it is still not delivered in time, the buyer may terminate and the earnest money is refunded."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if a buyer misses the title objection deadline in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Under Paragraph 6D, failing to object in time waives the right to object, except that the requirements in Schedule C of the Commitment are not waived. Timely objections get a 15-day Cure Period, provided the seller is not obligated to incur any expense, and the buyer then has 5 days after the Cure Period to terminate or waive. Silence is a waiver."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is an ag exemption in Texas, and what are rollback taxes?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "An ag exemption is not an exemption. It is a special appraisal that values qualifying land on its agricultural productivity instead of market value. Changing the use triggers a rollback: three years of additional tax equal to the difference between what was paid on ag value and what would have been paid on market value."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How many years is the Texas rollback tax?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Three. Tax Code 23.55 imposes an additional tax equal to the difference between the taxes imposed on the land for each of the three years preceding the year in which the change of use occurs and what would have been imposed at market value."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the interest rate on Texas rollback taxes?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Tax Code 23.55 does not impose a rollback interest rate for 1-d-1 open-space land. The statute provides only that the additional taxes incur the penalties and interest provided by law for ad valorem taxes if they are not paid by the delinquency deadline. Published 5 percent and 7 percent figures should not be relied on."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does land have to do to qualify for Texas ag valuation?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Its current principal use must be agricultural, carried on to the degree of intensity generally accepted in the area, and the land must have been in agricultural or timber production in five of the preceding seven years. The appraisal district sets and applies the local intensity standard."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I put raw land into wildlife management valuation in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Only if it already qualified. Wildlife management use requires land that was previously qualified open-space or timber land, used in at least three of seven specified ways to propagate a sustaining breeding, migrating or wintering population of indigenous wild animals. It is a conversion from ag valuation, not an independent starting point."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is an ETJ in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "An ETJ is a city's extraterritorial jurisdiction: unincorporated land just outside the city limits where the city keeps some regulatory authority under Texas Local Government Code chapter 42. Property there is not inside the city and not on the city's property tax roll, but some city rules still reach it. Boerne's short-term rental ordinance does."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do Boerne's short-term rental rules apply in the ETJ?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. Ordinance No. 2023-09 applies to the city limits and the ETJ. One piece of it does not travel: the safety inspection requirement applies to properties inside the city limits and does not apply to ETJ properties."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do you pay city property tax in a Texas ETJ?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "An ETJ parcel is not inside the city limits, so the city's property tax rate is not on its bill. The county, the school district and any special district covering the parcel still are. The entity list for a Kendall County parcel comes from Kendall Appraisal District."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between the ETJ and unincorporated county?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "ETJ land is unincorporated but adjacent to a city that retains some authority over it, notably subdivision platting. Land outside any city's ETJ is governed by the county alone, and Texas counties have narrower ordinance-making power than cities. Boerne's STR ordinance does not reach unincorporated Kendall County outside the city limits and ETJ."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I find out if a property is in Boerne's ETJ?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Confirm the jurisdiction for the specific address with the City of Boerne Planning Department at (830) 248-1501. ETJ boundaries change as a city grows and as state law changes, so a map screenshot or a listing remark is not a reliable answer."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is an HOA resale certificate in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "An HOA resale certificate is a Texas Property Code chapter 207 document the property owners' association prepares for a pending sale. It states assessments, unpaid amounts, known violations, insurance, pending lawsuits, reserves and transfer restrictions. The association must deliver it within 10 business days of a proper written request, prepared no earlier than 60 days before delivery."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How long does a Texas HOA have to deliver a resale certificate?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Within 10 business days of receiving a proper written request. That is up to two calendar weeks, which can be longer than the option period, so the request should go out immediately after the contract becomes effective."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How old can a Texas HOA resale certificate be?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The certificate must be prepared no earlier than 60 days before it is delivered. A certificate pulled for an earlier deal that fell through may be stale and unusable."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which TREC forms are used for a Texas HOA sale?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The Addendum for Property Subject to Mandatory Membership in a Property Owners Association, form 36-11 effective 07/01/2026, is attached to the contract. The certificate itself is Subdivision Information, Including Resale Certificate, form 37-5 effective 02/10/2014. Condominiums use the Condominium Resale Certificate, form 32-5."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much can a Texas HOA charge for a resale certificate?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Texas Property Code section 207.003(c) allows a property owners' association to charge a reasonable and necessary fee, not to exceed $375, to assemble, copy and deliver the required subdivision information. The $375 figure is a ceiling on a reasonable and necessary charge, not a flat entitlement. Transfer fees billed at closing are a separate item."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What should a buyer read first in an HOA resale certificate?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Known violations on that lot, any special assessment already levied but not yet due, the reserve figures, and the transfer restrictions. An open violation becomes the buyer's problem at closing, and a voted-but-unbilled special assessment does not appear on the listing."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is earnest money in Texas, and is it refundable?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Earnest money is a good-faith deposit the buyer delivers to the title company to be held in escrow. It is refundable if the buyer terminates during the option period or under a contract right such as the financing addendum. It is credited to the buyer at closing and cannot be released without mutual agreement or a court order."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much earnest money do I need in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "There is no statutory amount and TREC does not set one. Earnest money is a dollar blank in paragraph 5A of form 20-19 that the buyer and seller negotiate. Paragraph 5A(1) also allows additional earnest money delivered within a stated number of days after the effective date, so a buyer can stage the deposit."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Who holds earnest money in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The title company, acting as escrow agent, holds it in an escrow account. Paragraph 5A of form 20-19 directs the buyer to deliver it to the named Escrow Agent within 3 days after the Effective Date. The title company does not decide disputes and cannot release the funds to either party without mutual agreement or a court order."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if a Texas buyer delivers earnest money late?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Under paragraph 5C of TREC form 20-19 the seller may terminate the contract, exercise the paragraph 15 remedies, or both — but only by giving notice to the buyer before the buyer delivers the earnest money. A buyer who wires late but before that notice goes out has effectively cured it."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can a seller refuse to release earnest money in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A seller can decline to sign the release, and the funds then stay in escrow. Terminating the contract and receiving escrowed funds are separate events. A timely, written termination delivered exactly as the contract requires is what makes a refusal hard to defend; a disputed release goes to a Texas real estate attorney."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens to earnest money at closing in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "It is credited to the buyer on the settlement statement and applied toward the down payment and closing costs, reducing the amount the buyer wires at closing. If the buyer defaults instead, paragraph 15 lets the seller terminate and receive the earnest money as liquidated damages."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the 10 percent homestead cap in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Once a Texas home has a homestead exemption, the appraisal district cannot raise its net appraised value more than 10 percent per year, no matter how much the market moves. The cap does not transfer to a buyer. It resets to market value after a sale, which is why a new owner's tax bill often jumps."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does the Texas homestead cap transfer to the buyer?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. The cap ends with the sale. The new owner faces the district's market value, often informed by the price just paid, and must file their own homestead exemption to start a new cap. The seller's tax bill is not a forecast of the buyer's."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why does my notice show two different values?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A homesteaded property's notice shows the district's market value and a capped appraised value. Taxable value is the capped figure minus exemptions, and that is what the bill is built from. After several years of appreciation the two numbers can be far apart."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do rental properties get the 10 percent cap in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. The 10 percent cap requires a homestead exemption. Non-homestead property has had a temporary 20 percent circuit breaker under Tax Code 23.231 for tax years 2024, 2025 and 2026, which expires after the 2026 tax year unless the Legislature renews it."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does the homestead cap apply to new improvements?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Added value from new improvements such as square footage, a casita, a shop or a pool is generally taxed rather than absorbed under the cap. Ordinary repairs and maintenance are treated differently. The appraisal district decides how a specific project is classified."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the Amendment to Contract in a Texas real estate deal?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The Amendment to Contract is the TREC form used to change the terms of a contract that is already in effect: price, closing date, repairs, option period. It takes effect only when every party signs and it is delivered. The current version is TREC form 39-11, effective 07/01/2026. It changes a contract. It does not end one."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do you extend an option period in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "With item 7 of TREC form 39-11. It states an additional option fee as a dollar amount, a new expiration written as a date with 5:00 p.m. printed on the form, and a separate election on whether that additional option fee will or will not be credited to the Sales Price. It has to be signed by all parties and executed before the existing option period expires."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is the additional option fee credited to the sales price?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Only if the parties check that it is. Item 7 of TREC form 39-11 carries a separate will or will not election for whether the additional option fee is credited to the Sales Price. That election is independent of the original option fee, which the contract credits at closing."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can you check more than one box on the TREC amendment form?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. The header instruction reads check each applicable box, not check one only. A single amendment can change the sales price, the closing date and the option period at once. An item whose box is left unchecked is not a change, even if the blank underneath it was filled in."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do you move the financing approval deadline in a Texas contract?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Item 9 of TREC form 39-11 changes the date for the buyer to give written notice that the buyer cannot obtain Buyer Approval under the Third Party Financing Addendum. That is the Paragraph 2A deadline. It does not change Property Approval under Paragraph 2B, which runs to the 3rd day before the Closing Date."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can a real estate agent write custom language into a Texas amendment?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Item 10, Other Modifications, carries the printed warning that real estate brokers and sales agents are prohibited from practicing law, and the form's footer repeats it. License holders may fill in blanks but may not draft contract language. A leaseback, a holdback or a contingency the form does not contain is work for a Texas real estate attorney."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the BRRRR method, and does it work in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "BRRRR is buy, rehab, rent, refinance, repeat. It works in Texas, with one constitutional catch: if the property is the borrower's homestead, the refinance is a Section 50(a)(6) loan capped at 80 percent loan-to-value with a 12-day waiting period. A rental held as an investment is not the homestead and is not governed by 50(a)(6)."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does Section 50(a)(6) apply to an investment property in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. The Texas Constitution's home equity restrictions apply to the homestead. A rental held in the investor's name as an investment property is not the homestead and is not governed by Section 50(a)(6). The lender's own loan-to-value, seasoning and reserve requirements still apply, and those vary by lender and product."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why does the 12-month rule matter for BRRRR in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Only one Section 50(a)(6) loan may be secured by the homestead at a time, and 12 months must pass between home equity closings. An investor refinancing their own residence therefore cannot repeat the refinance step quickly, which is one reason BRRRR is usually structured on the investment side instead."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do property taxes change when a Texas property becomes a rental?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The property loses the residence homestead exemptions, including the $140,000 school district exemption, and it loses the 10 percent homestead appraisal cap. Non-homestead real property is instead covered by a 20 percent circuit breaker under Tax Code Section 23.231, which applies for tax years 2024 through 2026 and expires December 31, 2026 unless the Legislature renews it."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What should I confirm before starting a BRRRR deal in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Get the lender's actual cash-out terms and seasoning requirement in writing for the product you will use, confirm whether the property will be your homestead at the time of the refinance, and build a property tax line that assumes no homestead exemption, no 10 percent cap, and the circuit breaker's December 31, 2026 expiration."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the option period in a Texas real estate contract?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The option period is a negotiated window in the TREC contract during which a Texas buyer can terminate for any reason and get their earnest money back. The buyer pays a separate option fee to the title company within three days of the effective date. The fee is credited at closing and is not refunded on termination."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How long is the option period in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "There is no statutory length. The option period is a blank in the TREC contract that the buyer and seller negotiate. Seven to ten calendar days is common in practice, but the number is whatever the parties agree to."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "When is the option fee due in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Within three days after the effective date of the contract, delivered to the escrow agent, which is the title company, rather than to the seller. If the third day is a Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday, the deadline moves to the next qualifying day. Paragraph 5D of TREC form 20-19 provides that if the fee is not delivered in time, the buyer does not have the unrestricted right to terminate."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if the option fee amount is left blank in the Texas contract?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The buyer has no termination right. Paragraph 5D of TREC form 20-19 states that if no dollar amount is stated as the option fee, or if the buyer fails to deliver it within the time required, the buyer does not have the unrestricted right to terminate under paragraph 5. A blank is as fatal as a late payment."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How are option period days counted in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Calendar days. The effective date is day zero and each following day is numbered in sequence. The period ends at 5:00 p.m. local time on the final day. A ten-day option period starting November 1 ends at 5:00 p.m. on November 11."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is the option fee credited at closing?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. If the sale closes, the option fee is credited to the sales price. If the buyer terminates during the option period, the seller keeps the option fee and the buyer's earnest money is returned."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the Seller's Disclosure Notice in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The Seller's Disclosure Notice is a written notice Texas Property Code section 5.008 requires from a seller of residential property with not more than one dwelling unit. It discloses known condition, defects and flood history. If the seller delivers it after the contract is signed, the buyer may terminate for any reason within seven days after receiving it."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if the seller gives the disclosure after the contract is signed?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Texas Property Code section 5.008 gives the buyer the right to terminate the contract for any reason within seven days after receiving the notice. That right is independent of the option period, so a late disclosure can extend a buyer's exit window past the date the contract was signed."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does Paragraph 7B of the TREC contract say about the disclosure?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Paragraph 7B of TREC form 20-19 is a check-one-box-only election. Box 2 states that if the buyer does not receive the notice, the buyer may terminate at any time prior to closing with earnest money refunded, and that once the seller delivers it the buyer may terminate for any reason within 7 days after receiving it or prior to the closing, whichever first occurs. Closing can cut the seven days short."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Who is exempt from the Texas seller's disclosure?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Exemptions include transfers by court order, foreclosure, a trustee in bankruptcy, transfers between co-owners, transfers to a spouse or descendant, transfers by a fiduciary administering an estate, and new residences not previously occupied. The exemption is from delivering the form. It does not authorize misrepresentation or fraud."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does a Texas seller have to disclose a death in the house?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Section 5.008 states a seller or agent is not required to disclose a death on the property by natural causes, suicide, or an accident unrelated to the property's condition. A death that is related to the condition of the property is not covered by that carve-out."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does the Texas seller's disclosure cover flooding?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. SB 339 from the 86th Legislature added flood questions, mandatory for contracts executed on or after September 1, 2019. They cover FEMA 100-year and 500-year floodplain status, reservoir flood pool, whether the property flooded at least once in the past five years, flood repairs, and flood insurance proceeds."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the Texas homestead exemption in 2026?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The Texas homestead exemption removes part of your home's value from property taxation. For 2026, school districts must exempt $140,000 of a residence homestead's value, up from $100,000 after voters approved Proposition 13 in November 2025. You must own and occupy the home as your principal residence on January 1."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much is the Texas school district homestead exemption?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "School districts must grant a $140,000 residence homestead exemption for tax year 2026. The figure was raised from $100,000 by Proposition 13, approved by Texas voters in November 2025 and implemented through SB 4 of the 89th Legislature."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Who qualifies for a Texas homestead exemption?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "An owner who both owned and occupied the property as a principal residence on January 1 of the tax year. It applies to one home, not to rentals, second homes or vacant land. The application is filed with the county appraisal district."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "When is the Texas homestead exemption deadline?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Applications are due before May 1, commonly stated as April 30. They are filed with the appraisal district for the county where the property is located. There is no fee to file with the appraisal district."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Are there other homestead exemptions besides the school district one?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. Any taxing unit may adopt a local option exemption of up to 20 percent of appraised value with a $5,000 minimum. Counties that collect a farm-to-market road or flood control tax must give a $3,000 residence homestead exemption. Owners 65 or older or disabled get an additional $60,000 from the school district."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the Third Party Financing Addendum in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The Third Party Financing Addendum is the TREC form that makes a Texas contract conditional on the buyer's loan. It identifies the loan type and terms, and it gives the buyer a deadline to terminate if the lender will not approve the buyer. The current version is TREC form 40-11, effective 01/03/2025."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between Buyer Approval and Property Approval in TREC 40-11?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Paragraph 2 says financing approval is obtained only when both Buyer Approval and Property Approval are obtained. Buyer Approval, in Paragraph 2A, is about the borrower's assets, income and credit history, and it expires a negotiated number of days after the effective date. Property Approval, in Paragraph 2B, is about the house, including appraisal, insurability and lender required repairs, and it runs to the 3rd day before the Closing Date."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if the buyer does not terminate under Paragraph 2A in time?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The form states that if the buyer does not terminate under Paragraph 2A, the contract shall no longer be subject to the buyer obtaining Buyer Approval. The borrower-side contingency ends on that day count. A denial after that date gives the buyer no exit under 2A, and Paragraph 2 adds that time is of the essence and strict compliance with the time for performance is required."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does the buyer need a letter from the lender to terminate under the financing addendum?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. Both Paragraph 2A and Paragraph 2B require the buyer to give the seller two things: notice of termination, and a copy of a written statement from the lender setting forth the reasons for the lender's determination. A phone call from a loan officer does not satisfy that requirement."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does the Third Party Financing Addendum cover a low appraisal?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Appraisal falls under Paragraph 2B, Property Approval, which lists appraisal, insurability and lender required repairs. TREC also publishes a separate Addendum Concerning Right to Terminate Due to Lender's Appraisal, form 49-1 effective 03/01/2019, which addresses the appraisal outcome specifically and is often waived or partially waived in competitive offers."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What financing types can be checked on TREC form 40-11?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Paragraph 1 runs lettered A through G: conventional financing with a first and an optional second mortgage loan, a Texas Veterans Land Board loan, FHA insured financing, VA guaranteed financing, USDA guaranteed financing, reverse mortgage financing also known as a Home Equity Conversion Mortgage, and other financing. Each caps the interest rate and the Origination Charges shown on the buyer's Loan Estimate as a percentage of the loan, and each states the amount excluding any financed PMI, MIP or Funding Fee."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the TREC One to Four Family Residential Contract?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The One to Four Family Residential Contract (Resale) is the TREC promulgated form Texas license holders use to sell existing single-family homes, duplexes, triplexes and four-plexes. The current version is form 20-19, effective July 1, 2026. Condominiums, builder new construction and farm and ranch each use a different form."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the current version of the TREC 1-4 family contract?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Form 20-19, effective 07/01/2026. The PDF runs 12 pages and carries a 05-04-2026 footer date. TREC publishes the current form numbers and effective dates on its contracts page, and several other residential forms were updated on the same date."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can a Texas REALTOR write their own contract language?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Paragraph 23 of form 20-19 is titled CONSULT AN ATTORNEY BEFORE SIGNING and states that TREC rules prohibit real estate brokers and sales agents from giving legal advice. License holders are required to use TREC promulgated forms and TREC does not write custom contracts. Custom terms are the job of a Texas real estate attorney."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which TREC contract is used for a condo or new construction in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A condominium resale uses the Residential Condominium Contract (Resale), form 30-18. Builder new construction uses New Home Contract 24-20 for completed construction or 23-20 for incomplete construction. Vacant land uses the Unimproved Property Contract, 9-18, and acreage uses the Farm and Ranch Contract, 25-17."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How is a Texas real estate contract changed after signing?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "On the Amendment to Contract, TREC form 39-11, effective 07/01/2026, signed by both parties. Price changes, repair agreements, closing-date changes and option-period extensions belong there. Paragraph 7D of the contract warns against inserting general phrases such as 'subject to inspections' that do not identify specific repairs and treatments. Emails, texts and verbal agreements do not amend the contract."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are the main paragraphs of the TREC 20-19 contract?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Paragraph 5 is earnest money and the termination option, 6 is title policy and survey, 7 is property condition and the seller's disclosure election, 12 is settlement expenses and brokerage compensation, 13 is prorations, 14 is casualty loss, 15 is default, 18 is escrow, and 23 is consult an attorney before signing. The form runs 12 pages. Three paragraphs carry a printed check-one-box-only instruction — 6C for the survey, 7B for the seller's disclosure, and 7D for as-is versus listed repairs — and a blank or double-checked election is a real defect."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the VA funding fee, and who is exempt?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The VA funding fee is a one-time fee paid to the VA on most VA-guaranteed home loans, charged as a percentage of the loan amount. Veterans receiving VA compensation for a service-connected disability are exempt, as are several other groups. Exemption is documented with VA Form 26-8937, a current award letter, or a Veteran Service Center Manager letter."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does the VA funding fee pay for?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "It funds the VA guaranty, which is the portion of a private lender's loan that the VA backs. The guaranty does not exceed 25 percent of the loan. That backing is what allows VA loans to run without monthly mortgage insurance and, for a veteran with full entitlement, without a VA loan limit."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Am I exempt from the VA funding fee if I take retirement pay instead of disability compensation?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Potentially yes. The VA's published exemptions include veterans who are eligible to receive compensation for a service-connected disability but are receiving retirement pay or active-duty pay instead. Borrowers in that position often assume they do not qualify. Raise it with the loan officer at application and let the VA's records settle it."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What documents prove a VA funding fee exemption?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "VA Form 26-8937, Verification of VA Benefits; a current VA award letter dated within the last 12 months; or a letter from a Veteran Service Center Manager. An old award letter does not satisfy the date requirement, so obtain a current one at the start of the file rather than the week of closing."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is the VA funding fee exemption the same as the Texas disabled veteran property tax exemption?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. They are separate and one does not grant the other. The funding fee exemption is federal and applies to the loan. Texas Tax Code sections 11.22 and 11.131 govern disabled veteran property tax exemptions, filed with the county appraisal district — the Kendall Appraisal District for a property in Boerne or Kendall County."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is title insurance in Texas, and why is the price the same everywhere?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Texas title insurance premium rates are promulgated by the Texas Department of Insurance, so the premium for a given policy amount is identical at every title company in Texas. Shopping title companies is about service, escrow fees and closing experience, not premium price. The current rate schedule took effect March 1, 2026."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can you shop for a cheaper title company in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Not on premium. The premium is set by the Texas Department of Insurance and is the same at every title company for a given policy amount. Escrow and closing fees, ancillary charges, and how competently a company clears a difficult title issue do differ, and those are worth comparing."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between an owner's policy and a lender's policy in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The owner's policy protects the buyer's ownership interest against covered defects that existed before the policy date. The lender's policy protects the lender's lien in the amount of the loan and does not protect the buyer's equity. A buyer with only a lender's policy has no coverage of their own."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Who pays for title insurance in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "It is negotiated in the contract, not fixed by law. Paragraph 6A of TREC form 20-19 makes it a checkbox: the owner policy is furnished at Seller's or Buyer's expense. Custom in much of Texas is that the seller pays for the owner's policy and the buyer pays for the lender's policy, but custom varies by market and moves in competitive situations."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the survey exception on a Texas title policy?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "An exception excluding anything a survey would have shown: discrepancies, conflicts, shortages in area or boundary lines, encroachments, protrusions and overlapping improvements. Paragraph 6A(8) of TREC form 20-19 makes it a checkbox: the exception will not be amended, or it will be amended to read 'shortages in area' at the buyer's or seller's expense. Amending it requires a survey the title company and lender accept."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What should I know about septic systems and water wells in the Texas Hill Country?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A Hill Country property on a septic system and a private well carries due diligence a city-connected house does not. The buyer is responsible for inspecting the on-site sewage facility, testing and evaluating the well, and gathering permits and maintenance records, all inside the option period. TREC form 61-0 covers groundwater and surface water rights."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does a home inspection cover the septic system and the well?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. A general inspection under the TREC Property Inspection Report, form REI 7-6, is not a septic inspection and does not test water. A septic or OSSF inspector and a water test are separate items that have to be booked separately, and both have to fit inside the option period."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What documents should a buyer request on a well and septic property?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The OSSF permit and as-built site plan, the aerobic system maintenance contract and service records, pumping records, the well report or driller's log, pump service records, water test results, and any shared well agreement. A shared well agreement is a legal document and should be reviewed by a Texas real estate attorney."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Who regulates groundwater in Kendall County, Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The Cow Creek Groundwater Conservation District. It monitors conditions using roughly 42 monitor wells, stream gauges on Cibolo Creek and the Guadalupe River, and more than 100 rain gauge sites, and it sets drought stages that carry pumping restrictions. Current stage and rules should be checked at ccgcd.org."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is TREC form 61-0?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The Seller's Disclosure about Groundwater and Surface Water Rights, effective 07/01/2026. Water rights on a rural Texas property are not automatically what a buyer assumes, and the form puts the seller's statements about them in writing. It is a disclosure, not a substitute for inspection."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What surprises out-of-state buyers about buying a house in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The biggest surprises are the option period, which lets a Texas buyer terminate for any reason; that no attorney is required and the title company runs closing; that title insurance premiums are set by the state, so they are identical everywhere; and that annual reappraisal replaces a state income tax."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do you need a lawyer to buy a house in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. No attorney is required to close a residential transaction in Texas. The title company acts as escrow agent, runs the title search, issues the title commitment and policy, prepares closing documents and disburses funds. Hiring an attorney is optional. Because license holders cannot draft contract language, custom terms still require a Texas real estate attorney."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does title insurance cost more at some Texas title companies?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Texas title insurance premium rates are promulgated by the Texas Department of Insurance, so the premium for a given policy amount is identical at every title company in the state. The current schedule is effective March 1, 2026. Compare title companies on service rather than premium."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if the seller gives the disclosure notice late in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Under Texas Property Code 5.008, if the contract is signed without the seller having given the required seller's disclosure notice, the buyer may terminate the contract for any reason within 7 days after receiving it. That right runs on its own clock, separate from the option period."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do I have to buy a new survey when I buy a house in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Not necessarily. If the lender and title company accept the seller's existing survey together with a T-47 notarized affidavit, or the T-47.1 declaration in lieu of affidavit introduced November 1, 2024, the buyer does not need a new survey and the policy's survey exception can be amended. A seller who has added a deck, pool, shed or fence since the survey cannot sign it."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "When does a Texas homestead exemption start for a new buyer?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "You must have owned and occupied the home as your principal residence on January 1 of the tax year, with an application deadline before May 1. A buyer closing in February does not get the exemption that year. The exemption is filed with the appraisal district, is not automatic, and does not transfer from the seller."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Who pays closing costs in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Who pays closing costs in Texas is negotiated in the contract, not fixed by law. Custom in much of Texas is that the seller pays the owner's title policy and the buyer pays lender and loan-related costs, but every line is negotiable. Your lender's Loan Estimate and the title company's fee sheet give the actual numbers."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does Texas law require the seller to pay the owner's title policy?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Paragraph 6A of TREC form 20-19 reads that Seller shall furnish the owner policy of title insurance at Seller's or Buyer's expense, with a checkbox for each. It is custom in much of Texas for the seller to pay it, not law, and in some markets the buyer does. It can be changed afterward on the Amendment to Contract, form 39-11."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What expenses does the TREC contract assign to each party in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Paragraph 12A(1) assigns the seller lien releases including prepayment penalties and recording fees, release of seller's loan liability, tax statements or certificates, preparation of deed, and one-half of the escrow fee. Paragraph 12A(2) assigns the buyer appraisal, loan application, origination, credit report and loan document fees, the loan title policy with lender endorsements, one-half of the escrow fee, prepaid flood and hazard insurance premiums and reserves, and PMI, the VA Loan Funding Fee or FHA MIP as required by the lender."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is real estate commission set by law in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Paragraph 12B of TREC form 20-19 states that brokerage compensation is not set by law and is fully negotiable, and that each party pays their own broker under separate written agreements. Optional checkboxes let one party contribute toward the other's broker compensation, but those contributions do not change either party's obligation under its own agreement."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can the seller pay the buyer's closing costs in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. A seller contribution is written into the contract and appears as a seller credit on the settlement statement. The limit is set by the buyer's loan program, not by the contract, and it varies by loan type, occupancy and down payment. The lender is the only source for that cap."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I find out my actual closing costs in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Two documents. The Loan Estimate from your lender itemizes loan costs and other costs and gives the estimated cash to close. The title company handling the transaction will produce an estimated settlement statement using the actual sales price, the actual tax rates for the property and the actual policy amount."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Who pays for the survey in a Texas real estate transaction?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Who pays for the survey is negotiated in the contract, not set by Texas law. The TREC contract has a paragraph where the parties choose. Custom in much of Texas is that the seller furnishes an existing survey with a T-47 affidavit, and the buyer pays for a new one if the existing survey is not acceptable."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does Texas law say the seller pays for the survey?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. No Texas statute assigns the survey cost. Paragraph 6C of TREC form 20-19 presents three mutually exclusive options under the instruction Check one box only, and paragraph 6A(8) separately allocates the cost of amending the survey exception. It is negotiable in the offer and can be changed later on the Amendment to Contract, form 39-11."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can a buyer in Texas use the seller's old survey?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Often, yes. Paragraph 6C(1) of TREC form 20-19 has the seller furnish the existing survey together with a T-47 Affidavit or T-47.1 Declaration, both named in the contract. If the title company and lender accept the package, no new survey is required. Some lenders require a current survey regardless."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if the title company will not accept the existing survey?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Paragraph 6C(1) says the buyer obtains a new survey at Seller's or Buyer's expense — a separate checkbox inside that option — no later than 3 days prior to the Closing Date. That is different from the seller simply failing to furnish the survey and affidavit, in which case the new survey is at Seller's expense with no election."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if a Texas buyer elects to get the survey and then does not?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Paragraph 6C(2) states that if the buyer fails to obtain the survey, the buyer may not terminate under Paragraph 2B of the Third Party Financing Addendum because the survey was not obtained. The buyer forfeits the financing exit and remains in the contract with earnest money exposed."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much does a survey cost in Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "It is set by the individual surveyor and varies with acreage, terrain, how well existing corners are marked, and how much record research the tract needs. A platted subdivision lot and a large rural tract are not comparable jobs. Get a quote from a licensed Texas surveyor for the specific property."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why are homes in Boerne, Texas so expensive?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Boerne prices are driven by structural scarcity, not hype. Kendall County grew 20.3 percent from 2020 to 2025 on domestic migration, Boerne ISD has six straight A ratings, Hill Country terrain and groundwater regulation limit how densely land can be developed, and I-10 puts San Antonio jobs 31 miles away."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the median home price in Boerne, Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Published figures vary by source, ZIP code and month. Zillow reported an average Boerne home value of roughly $554,983 as of September 2026, which is a model estimate rather than closed-sale data. Ask a local agent for current MLS sold figures for your specific ZIP and price band."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does water availability affect Boerne home prices?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, indirectly. Boerne draws water from Canyon Lake through the Guadalupe-Blanco River Authority, from Boerne City Lake, and from Trinity Group aquifer wells. Groundwater is regulated countywide by the Cow Creek Groundwater Conservation District, which limits how densely land can be developed and constrains new supply."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do Boerne ISD school ratings push home prices up?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Boerne ISD has earned an A rating from the Texas Education Agency for six consecutive years and scored 92 points in the 2024-2025 ratings. A rating record that long concentrates relocating-family demand inside district boundaries, which shows up in price."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Are Boerne property taxes included in the sale price comparison?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. The City of Boerne 2025 rate is 0.4716 per $100 and the Boerne ISD 2025 rate is 1.0109 per $100. Texas reappraises at market value annually, so a seller's current tax bill can understate a buyer's future bill. Have a lender underwrite the payment on an unexempted valuation."
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why did my Texas property taxes go up?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Your tax bill is appraised value times the combined rate of every taxing unit that covers your property, minus exemptions. It rises when the appraisal rises, when any single entity raises its rate, when an exemption is lost or was never filed, or when the 10 percent homestead cap resets to market value after a sale."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why did my taxes go up the year after I bought the house?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The prior owner's 10 percent homestead cap and exemptions do not transfer to a buyer. The property is reappraised at market value and the new owner must file their own homestead exemption, which requires owning and occupying the home as a principal residence on January 1. The seller's old tax bill is not a forecast of yours."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are the 2025 tax rates in Boerne, Texas?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Per $100 of taxable value for 2025: Boerne ISD 1.0109, City of Boerne 0.4716, and Kendall County 0.377. That totals 1.8595 before any MUD, water district or groundwater district that may also appear on a given parcel's bill. Kendall County adopted 0.373 for 2026."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How is a Texas property tax bill calculated?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Appraised value minus exemptions, divided by 100, times the tax rate — calculated separately for each taxing unit covering the parcel, then added together. The appraisal district sets value and exemptions; each taxing unit's elected board sets its own rate."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does the 20 percent circuit breaker on non-homestead property expire?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. The Tax Code 23.231 circuit breaker applies to tax years 2024, 2025 and 2026 and expires after the 2026 tax year unless the Legislature renews it. No application is required and the district applies it automatically, but the owner must have held the property at least one full calendar year."
      }
    }
  ]
}
